    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p23-q14-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the area of the largest orange triangle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac32$ square units.",
          "worked": "The largest orange triangle has perpendicular sides of lengths $3$ and $1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}A_1&=\\frac12\\times3\\times1\\\\A_1&=\\frac32\\text{ square units}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q14-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the ratio of the area of the second largest orange triangle to the area of the largest orange triangle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac58$.",
          "worked": "A side of the second square is the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle with legs $1$ and $3$. Let its length be $s$.\n$$\\begin{align*}s^2&=1^2+3^2\\\\s^2&=10.\\end{align*}$$\nCorresponding orange triangles are similar, so their area ratio equals the square of the side-length ratio:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{A_2}{A_1}&=\\frac{s^2}{4^2}\\\\\\frac{A_2}{A_1}&=\\frac{10}{16}\\\\\\frac{A_2}{A_1}&=\\frac58.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q14-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, find the total area of all the orange triangles.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p23-q14-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "4",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " square units.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p23-q14-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Sum the geometric series with first term ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\frac32",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and ratio ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\frac58",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The areas form a geometric series with first term $\\frac32$ and common ratio $\\frac58$. Since $\\left|\\frac58\\right|<1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}S&=\\frac{a}{1-r}\\\\S&=\\frac{\\frac32}{1-\\frac58}\\\\S&=\\frac{\\frac32}{\\frac38}\\\\S&=4\\text{ square units}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 27
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q14-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Use the diagram to give an argument that\n$$\\frac32\\left(1+\\frac58+\\frac{5^2}{8^2}+\\cdots\\right)=4.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p23-q14-d-short-reason",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Four congruent spirals fill the square, so the orange spiral has one quarter of its area.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p23-q14-d-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\frac14\\times4^2=4",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " square units.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The four coloured spirals are congruent under quarter-turns about the square's centre. The remaining inner square has area tending to zero because each nested square has $\\frac58$ of the preceding area. Thus the spirals fill the square of area $4^2=16$, and the orange spiral occupies one quarter of it. Its triangle areas are $\\frac32,\\frac32\\left(\\frac58\\right),\\frac32\\left(\\frac58\\right)^2,\\ldots$. Therefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac32\\left(1+\\frac58+\\frac{5^2}{8^2}+\\cdots\\right)&=\\frac14\\times16\\\\\\frac32\\left(1+\\frac58+\\frac{5^2}{8^2}+\\cdots\\right)&=4.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      }
    ]
